# tools/cr/crypto_slhdsa_thash_witness.rish -- rung DISC0 of the SLH-DSA ladder: the ADDRESS structure and the TWEAKABLE HASH FAMILY every other
# rung of a hash-based signature is built from, authored in Rye on our own SHAKE-256 (Season G -- Cryptography -- Rye-native, audit-ready).
#
# WHY THIS IS RUNG ZERO. SLH-DSA carries no field arithmetic and no curve -- it is hashes all the way down, and the only thing keeping one hash
# from colliding with another in a different role is the 32-byte ADDRESS mixed into every call. An address names where in the structure a hash
# lives: which hypertree layer, which tree, which one-time key pair, which Winternitz chain, which step along it. A field written one byte off
# does not merely compute the wrong answer -- it silently merges two domains FIPS 205 keeps apart. So the address is built and proven first.
#
# WHAT THIS WITNESS PROVES ALONE, without any vendored source: every field lands on the byte the standard names; every byte no field names stays
# zero, so an address carries no accidental payload; the subtree and key-pair carries keep exactly what they should and reset the rest; DOMAIN
# SEPARATION holds, shown by hashing one message at two addresses differing in the type byte alone and requiring different bytes out; and both
# bounds refuse with a named error rather than truncating.
#
# Parity against the vendored PQClean is its own rung -- tools/cr/crypto_slhdsa_thash_parity_witness.rish -- kept separate on purpose so this one
# runs in the crypto suite with no dependency on a submodule being checked out.
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread. Ladder: DISC (waymark seated 20260821.023219).
# Design read: active-designing/20260821-022912_slhdsa-vendored-oracle-and-both-legs-kept.md
# Kin: crypto/shake.rye (our SHAKE-256, GREEN against Zig's std.crypto and FIPS 202 -- this rung authors no new sponge, only the addressing and
# framing around one) - tools/cr/crypto_slhdsa_thash_parity_witness.rish (the PQClean diff).
#
# HONEST SCOPE -- purely local. Every seed is a test constant; no real key, no signature at this rung, no network, no funds, no device.
# Constant-time timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon: nothing here branches on secret DATA, yet a timing CLAIM wants measurement.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_slhdsa_thash_witness.rish

say "crypto-slhdsa-thash: Language EN -- the first rung of the SLH-DSA ladder is not a signature but an ADDRESS. A hash-based scheme is correct exactly insofar as every hash knows which role it is playing, and the 32-byte address is what tells it. Building this alone, before any signature exists, is what lets a fault be named at the field that caused it rather than found later as a signature that simply does not verify."
say "crypto-slhdsa-thash: Style Radiant -- one function where the standard appears to give three. For the SHAKE instances, F, H, and T_l are all SHAKE256 over the public seed, the address, and the message, so this module authors ONE thash over a block count rather than three near-identical bodies. FIPS 205's simple instances define them that way and PQClean's own C is the same single function; naming it once is how a reader sees that truth rather than inferring it from copies."
say "crypto-slhdsa-thash: Lens TAME -- every bound is named at construction and refused at the edge: max_blocks 67, the widest a WOTS+ public key compresses at, and a message whose length must equal the block count it claims. Both refusals carry named errors and are proven in the selftest rather than described. Domain separation is proven by doing -- two hashes differing in the type byte alone must differ in output. Purely local -- no real key, no signature yet, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/slhdsa_thash"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/slhdsa_thash.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-slhdsa-thash: build failed -- ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/slhdsa_thash.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-slhdsa-thash: selftest exited non-zero -- see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-slhdsa-thash: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-slhdsa-thash' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-slhdsa-thash: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-slhdsa-thash line"
